{"id": "MX-C-0005", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "The two Mathlib checkouts available to Stream 0 have different built subsets and neither is complete. MechanicaFluidorum/lean_src has Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Deriv and Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.{Add,Mul}; RajMathRecovery/dualscale/lean does not. MX-A-0011 does not compile against the second and compiles on the first attempt against the first. Which tree LEAN_ENV_DIR resolves to therefore determines what Stream 0 can prove, with no diagnostic separating a false statement from an unbuilt module. Verified 2026-08-13 by compiling the same file against both. This is concrete evidence for PLAN.md K1 and partially reverses the K1 downgrade recorded in docs/OWNER_BRIEF.md D5, which was argued only from Stream 1's perspective.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "scripts/check_footprints.py#DEFAULT_ENV_CANDIDATES + lean/Mathesis/Applications/LotkaVolterraFlow.lean (module docstring)", "supports": [], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-C-0006", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Cross-stream status survey, 2026-08-13, each item verified directly by this session rather than taken from a subagent report. (a) RajMathRecovery/NAMAGIRI.lean, a standalone file at the repository root, defines Real := Float and Complex := (Float x Float), defines uniformBoundedness and topologicalFracture as Prop := True, and proves hypothesis_U_bound and prevents_singularity by trivial, with docstrings claiming respectively to prove Hypothesis U and that the singular set is forced into discrete non-communicating states. The file is imported by nothing and is not in any lakefile, so it contaminates no build. (b) TNN-UniversModel/specs/TNN_Invariants.lean line 46 contains sorry in energy_conservation, while dashboard/backend/main.py contains the string literal 'PROVEN (Zero-Sorry)' fifteen times as hardcoded data, not computed from any check. (c) RajMathRecovery/dualscale/lean excluding vendored Mathlib contains 2 sorry and 34 axiom, confirming the earlier count in MX-C-0003.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "docs/FOUNDATIONS.md#11 + docs/designs/RECONCILIATION.md#4", "supports": ["MX-C-0003"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-B-0007", "tier": "B", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "UC6, the refutation case. Three proposed conservation laws are refuted by explicit exact counterexamples: that each body's kinetic energy is individually conserved in a 1-D elastic collision (m=(1,3), u=(4,0): body 1 goes from 16 to 4 while the total is conserved); that Lotka-Volterra conserves total population x+y (at alpha,beta,gamma,delta=(2,1,1,1), (x,y)=(3,1), d(x+y)/dt = 5); and that Reff is multiplicative in R (alpha=4: Reff(4,4)=4 but Reff(4,1)*Reff(4,4)=16). A fourth proposal of the same kind, that a 1-D elastic collision preserves relative speed, is TRUE and survives 192 exact cases; the harness fails if it is refuted. A refuter that refutes everything is as useless as one that refutes nothing.", "evidence_kind": "exact_harness", "artifact": "tests/tier_b_refutations.py", "supports": ["MX-A-0007", "MX-A-0005"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-C-0007", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Owner decisions recorded 2026-08-14. (1) Tier L is ADOPTED: the five-tier order X<C<L<B<A is the programme's citation-strength notation, and Stream 5's literature-backed results migrate B->L. (2) The SPEC-STREAM0 R3.4 two-consumer rule is WAIVED for Mathesis.TierCalculus, on the record: no Lean module imports it and no external stream's CI calls it, so by a strict reading it should not be in the library. The waiver rationale accepted by the owner is that the Python and Rust ledger checkers implement the calculus and consume it in substance even though no Lean file imports the module, and that the module is cheap to maintain (Mathlib-free, ~2s build, zero dependencies). The counter-argument, recorded so it is not lost: 'exempt because it is cheap' is the reasoning every abstraction uses to survive a YAGNI rule, and this waiver removes the only scheduled forcing function for adoption. (3) Stream 0 will open pull requests in sibling repositories rather than only reporting; see MX-C-0008 for what that turned out to be possible for.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "docs/OWNER_BRIEF.md", "supports": ["MX-C-0001", "MX-C-0002"], "audited_by": "owner decision 2026-08-14", "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-A-0012", "tier": "A", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "The abstract self-dual bound and its instances. For reals with 0 <= x, 0 <= y and C <= x*y, sqrt C <= max x y; dually min x y <= sqrt C when x*y <= C, and an exactly conserved product sandwiches sqrt C between the two dual factors. The additive twin is two-term AM-GM. For x > 0, x = C/x iff x = sqrt C. Instances proved as corollaries: Reff(alpha,R) = max(R, alpha/R) >= sqrt alpha; the same bound cast to naturals; the Wilson EOQ inventory bound 2*sqrt(D*K*h/2) <= D*K/Q + h*Q/2, together with its attainment at the self-dual lot size Q = sqrt(2*D*K/h), which makes it the optimal lot size rather than merely a true inequality. If sinh(2K)*sinh(2K) = 1 and K > 0 then K = log(1 + sqrt 2)/2. Four non-vacuity witnesses in both polarities: the A.1 bound is attained at the self-dual point, is strict away from it, is FALSE if the sign hypotheses are dropped (x = -1, y = -4 satisfies the product hypothesis and refutes the conclusion), and the sinh fixed-point hypothesis is satisfiable, so the last theorem is not an implication with an empty antecedent. 14 declarations, all footprints [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound].", "evidence_kind": "lean_axioms", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Duality/SelfDual.lean", "supports": [], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-C-0009", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "The physics reading of MX-A-0012's sinh_selfDual_coupling, filed separately because it is NOT what the Lean theorem proves. Kramers-Wannier duality for the 2-D Ising model pairs couplings by sinh(2K)*sinh(2K*) = 1; imposing K* = K yields the theorem's hypothesis, and the value log(1 + sqrt 2)/2 it returns is Onsager's critical coupling. The step from 'the self-dual point' to 'the critical point' requires the further premise that the transition is unique, which Kramers-Wannier (1941) assumed and Onsager (1944) proved. That premise appears nowhere in the Lean file, which contains no lattice, no partition function and no duality map. The theorem is therefore the sinh case of the abstract fixed-point lemma, and the Ising identification is an argument at Tier C pending a Tier L citation with a quoted theorem statement (LL-7). The module was renamed from kramers_wannier_self_dual to sinh_selfDual_coupling for this reason.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Duality/SelfDual.lean (header, WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CONTAIN)", "supports": ["MX-A-0012"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-C-0010", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Review of an externally proposed Duality/SelfDual module, 2026-08-14, verified by compiling rather than by reading. The proposal shipped uncompiled and stated so. Compiling it against Mathlib found two defects: eoq_lower_bound had a trailing ring after a field_simp that already closed the goal (No goals to be solved), and kramers_wannier_self_dual called Real.sinh_pos, which does not exist; the lemma is Real.sinh_pos_iff. Both theorem names were nevertheless defined, each with sorryAx in its footprint, so a source-level sorry grep would have reported the file clean. Seven of the nine proposed theorems were correct as written. Two further findings were statement-adequacy failures that no gate detects: the module named a theorem after Donoho-Stark whose Fourier content was its undischarged hypothesis, and named another after Kramers-Wannier while containing no Ising model. Two supporting citations offered by the proposal were checked and are real and correctly attributed: Hirschel, Vadakkumbatt, Baker, Schweizer, Sankey, Singh and Davis, Superfluid helium ultralight dark matter detector, Phys. Rev. D 109, 095011 (2024); and Godfrin, Beauvois, Sultan, Krotscheck, Dawidowski, Fak and Ollivier, Dispersion relation of Landau elementary excitations and thermodynamic properties of superfluid 4He, Phys. Rev. B 103, 104516 (2021). Neither is yet citable at Tier L: LL-7 requires a quoted theorem or measurement statement on file, and only titles and author lists have been verified.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "LL.md#LL-17 + lean/Mathesis/Duality/SelfDual.lean", "supports": ["MX-A-0012"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-B-0008", "tier": "B", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Exact-arithmetic companion to MX-A-0012, closing the only Lean module in the repository that had none. Because sqrt C is irrational for almost every rational C, the bound is checked in its squared form C <= max(x,y)^2 for 0 <= x, 0 <= y, which is equivalent over the nonnegatives, exactly checkable over the rationals, and is the form in which the bound is used; the float ban forces the reformulation, as it did for Kepler III. Checked: the squared bound over 700 exact cases; attainment at the self-dual point over 10; the Reff instance over 100 cases with equality occurring exactly twice and only where R^2 = alpha; EOQ optimality over 116 parameter families whose 2DK/h is a perfect rational square, 1160 comparisons, with the two dual costs verified to balance exactly at Q*; and the algebraic core of the sinh fixed point, where the harness fails if it does not see both roots of s^2 = 1, since otherwise it is not testing the sign hypothesis. 5 negative controls, all demonstrated to fire. Two defects were planted and observed to fail the harness with exit code 1 before it was filed (LL-3): replacing max by min in the squared bound produced 242 failures, and neutering one negative control was caught by the control-fired check.", "evidence_kind": "exact_harness", "artifact": "tests/tier_b_selfdual.py", "supports": ["MX-A-0012", "MX-A-0005"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-A-0013", "tier": "A", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Parseval on ZMod N for Mathlib's unnormalised discrete Fourier transform ZMod.dft: for f : ZMod N -> C with NeZero N, the sum over k of dft f k times the conjugate of dft f k equals N times the sum over j of f j times the conjugate of f j, and in real norms the sum over k of the squared norm of dft f k equals N times the sum over j of the squared norm of f j. Mathlib does not have this. Mathlib/Analysis/Fourier/ZMod.lean defines dft as a linear equivalence but contains no norm or inner-product lemma at all, and the Parseval results Mathlib does carry are for AddCircle and for the continuous Fourier transform. The proof here is built from character orthogonality, AddChar.sum_mulShift specialised to ZMod.stdAddChar via ZMod.isPrimitive_stdAddChar, together with an expansion lemma turning the modulus squared into a double sum whose character argument is a difference. The factor N rather than 1 is the unnormalised convention ZMod.dft uses, and is what makes the Donoho-Stark bound N. Before estimating, Analysis/Fourier/ZMod.olean and Analysis/Fourier/FiniteAbelian/Orthogonality.olean were verified to be BUILT in the resolved environment, per MX-C-0005, since a missing module and a false statement report identically.", "evidence_kind": "lean_axioms", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Duality/Uncertainty.lean#dft_parseval,dft_parseval_norm,sum_stdAddChar,conj_stdAddChar,dft_mul_conj_expand", "supports": [], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-A-0014", "tier": "A", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "The Donoho-Stark uncertainty principle on ZMod N, discharging TARGET T-DS. For nonzero f : ZMod N -> C, N is at most the product of the cardinality of the support of f and the cardinality of the support of its discrete Fourier transform. A signal and its spectrum cannot both be concentrated. Proved from MX-A-0013 together with Cauchy-Schwarz over the support in the form Mathlib's sq_sum_le_card_mul_sum_sq. The corollary sqrt_le_max_support then gives that the larger of the two supports is at least the square root of N, which is MX-A-0012's abstract self-dual bound A.1 instantiated at C = N; this is the first time that bound has been applied to a product hypothesis that is proved rather than assumed. Two non-vacuity witnesses: the hypothesis f nonzero is load-bearing, since for f = 0 both supports are empty and the product is 0 which is less than N; and the bound is attained at N = 1, so it cannot be improved in general. This is a theorem about ZMod N and finite sums and says nothing about quantum mechanics or measurement; the shared name with the Heisenberg principle is an analogy between two inequalities and is not licensed by this file.", "evidence_kind": "lean_axioms", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Duality/Uncertainty.lean#donoho_stark,norm_dft_sq_le,sqrt_le_max_support,witness_donoho_stark_needs_ne_zero,witness_donoho_stark_tight", "supports": ["MX-A-0013", "MX-A-0012"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-C-0011", "tier": "C", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Decision recorded 2026-08-14 on the duplicate Reff proof, and a correction to this session's own estimate. (1) Reff is now proved twice: the bespoke proof in lean/Mathesis/Scale/Reff.lean consolidated from Stream 5, and Reff_ge_sqrt_of_selfDual in Duality/SelfDual.lean as a one-line instance of the abstract bound. BOTH SURVIVE, by owner decision. The rationale is that two independent kernel proofs of one statement is Gate 3's differential philosophy applied inside Lean: if a future edit breaks one and not the other, the disagreement is information rather than a silent regression. The cost is about two seconds of build time. The same trade is made deliberately in Uncertainty.lean, where sqrt_le_max_support repeats SelfDual A.1 rather than importing it, because Gate 2 compiles each module standalone with no lakefile and there is no cross-module import available. (2) Correction: this session estimated the Donoho-Stark target as multi-day work on the ground that Mathlib has no Parseval for ZMod.dft, and recommended dropping it. The premise was true but the estimate was wrong, because the two hard ingredients were already present in Mathlib: AddChar.sum_mulShift for character orthogonality and sq_sum_le_card_mul_sum_sq for Cauchy-Schwarz. The remaining work was assembly and it completed in one session. The owner overrode the recommendation and was correct. The lesson is that the absence of the headline theorem from a library is not evidence about the cost of proving it; the cost is set by which ingredients are present, and that is a separate question which must be asked separately.", "evidence_kind": "argument", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Duality/Uncertainty.lean (header) + lean/Mathesis/Scale/Reff.lean", "supports": ["MX-A-0005", "MX-A-0012", "MX-A-0014"], "audited_by": "owner decision 2026-08-14", "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-A-0015", "tier": "A", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Phase winding around a discrete loop is quantized. For a loop modelled as ZMod L with L nonzero, and psi : ZMod L -> C nonvanishing at every site, the principal phase increments arg(psi(k+1)/psi(k)) summed over the loop equal 2*pi times an integer. The integer is not assumed: it appears because the increments exponentiate to a product that telescopes to 1, since k -> k+1 is a bijection of ZMod L, and because exp(theta*I) = 1 forces theta to lie in 2*pi*Z. Two non-vacuity witnesses on the same four-site loop differing in exactly one amplitude: full4 = (1, i, -1, -i) has every increment equal to i and total 2*pi, so the winding number is 1 and the theorem is not vacuously about n = 0; and hole4, the same loop with the single site at index 2 replaced by 0, has total pi, which is not an integer multiple of 2*pi, so the nonvanishing hypothesis is load-bearing rather than bookkeeping. This is the intended Tier A core of QF-A-0001, staged in Stream 0 before the QuantumFluids stream exists. It is a theorem about complex numbers. It contains no fluid, no Planck constant and no circulation; the reading that psi is a superfluid order parameter and that the loop integral of v equals n*h/m is a world claim, is Tier C, and will be filed as QF-C-0001. The file is named Winding rather than Circulation for exactly that reason, which is LL-17 applied before the fact rather than after.", "evidence_kind": "lean_axioms", "artifact": "lean/Mathesis/Applications/Winding.lean#winding_is_integer,prod_shift,prod_ratio_eq_one,witness_winding_one,witness_hole_sum,witness_needs_nonvanishing", "supports": [], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-B-0009", "tier": "B", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Exact-arithmetic companion to MX-A-0015. Complex.arg is transcendental and cannot be evaluated exactly, so the float ban forces a reformulation that needs no angles at all: partition the nonzero Gaussian rationals into four quadrants by the signs of the real and imaginary parts, and accumulate quadrant transitions mapped into minus one, zero and plus one, with the half-turn transition rejected as genuinely ambiguous in direction. The winding number is the total divided by four, and the theorem's content becomes the divisibility statement that the total is always divisible by four. Everything is integer arithmetic on exact Fraction coordinates; there is no angle, no pi and no rounding. Checked: quantization over 56 admissible loops with 48 correctly refused as inadmissible, and the harness fails if every loop is skipped; the witnessed winding values, where the unit four-site loop gives 1, matching the number the Lean kernel proves for the same loop, its reversal gives -1, a double traversal gives 2, a rational octagon gives 1 and a loop confined to one quadrant gives 0; rotation invariance over 16 rotations; and additivity under repeated traversal over 12 cases. 5 negative controls, all demonstrated to fire, including that an OPEN path is not quantized, which shows the divisibility is not an artifact of the encoding but comes from the loop closing, the harness twin of prod_ratio_eq_one in the Lean proof. Two defects were planted in copies and observed to exit 1 before filing (LL-3): swapping two quadrants broke quantization, and deleting the half-turn rejection was caught by its negative control.", "evidence_kind": "exact_harness", "artifact": "tests/tier_b_winding.py", "supports": ["MX-A-0015"], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
{"id": "MX-L-0001", "tier": "L", "stream": "Mathesis", "statement": "Chebotarev's theorem on roots of unity, retrieved and quoted 2026-08-15 from P. E. Frenkel, Simple proof of Chebotarev's theorem on roots of unity, arXiv:math/0312398v3, 1 July 2004. Quoted theorem statement: for any sets I, J contained in F_p with equal cardinality, the matrix (omega^(ij)) indexed by i in I and j in J has non-zero determinant, where p is prime and omega a primitive p-th root of unity. The paper's two lemmas are also quoted verbatim: Lemma 1, Z[omega]/(1-omega) = F_p; and Lemma 2, for a nonzero g in F_p[x] of degree less than p, the multiplicity of any nonzero a in F_p as a root of g is strictly less than the number of non-zero coefficients of g. The proof proceeds by reducing coefficients modulo (1-omega) and applying Lemma 2, then infinite descent. This row is Tier L because it is a citation to a quoted theorem statement, which is the cap for evidence_kind citation; it is NOT proved in this repository and no Lean file contains it. Recorded because SelfDual.lean's TARGET T-TAO had been written from memory and carried an LL-6 retrieval marker. IMPORTANT CAVEAT for anyone planning to use this: Frenkel ASSERTS, with attribution to Tao, that the theorem is equivalent to the inequality |supp f| + |supp f-hat| >= p+1 for nonzero f on F_p, but does not prove that equivalence. A Lean proof of Chebotarev therefore does not by itself yield T-TAO; the bridge is a separate theorem in a separate paper (T. Tao, An uncertainty principle for cyclic groups of prime order, arXiv:math.CA/0308286) which has NOT been retrieved, and filing T-TAO as following from Chebotarev without that pass would be citing an attribution rather than a theorem statement, which is LL-7.", "evidence_kind": "citation", "artifact": "docs/TARGET_CHEBOTAREV.md", "supports": [], "audited_by": null, "supersedes": null}
